-- title:
--   The resolution morphism is locally of finite type and quasi-compact
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a commutative ring, $t \in W$ an element and $e$ a natural number. For a parameter $s \in W$ write $\mathrm{CrossingQuotient}\,W\,s$ for the quotient of the polynomial ring $W[X_0,X_1]$ in two variables by the ideal generated by $X_0X_1 - s$, and $\mathrm{crossingScheme}\,s = \operatorname{Spec}$ of that ring. The scheme $\mathrm{Resolution}\;t\;e$ is the colimit of the diagram `glueDiagram t e` indexed by the gluing poset `GlueIndex e`, whose objects are the charts `glueObj t e` and whose maps are the transition morphisms `glueMap t e`; the morphism $\mathrm{Resolution.toCrossing}\;t\;e \colon \mathrm{Resolution}\;t\;e \to \mathrm{crossingScheme}\,(t^e)$ is the unique map out of this colimit induced by the cocone `crossingCocone t e` with vertex $\mathrm{crossingScheme}\,(t^e)$ and components `crossingCoconeApp t e`. The theorem asserts the conjunction of two statements about this morphism: it is locally of finite type, and it is quasi-compact. No condition on $W$, $t$ or $e$ beyond the stated ones is imposed; in particular $e = 0$ is allowed.
--
--   This is the finite-type half of the assertion that the resolution of the affine scheme $\operatorname{Spec} W[u,v]/(uv - t^e)$ obtained by gluing $e$ copies of $\operatorname{Spec} W[x,y]/(xy-t)$ maps properly to its base. It is used by [`MvPolynomial.CrossingQuotient.Resolution.isProper_toCrossing`](thm.html#MvPolynomial.CrossingQuotient.Resolution.isProper_toCrossing), where it is combined with separatedness and universal closedness.
